# Line-ending policy. P-124: nothing declared one, and eight tracked files carried both
# conventions at once. The committed bytes are the convention, and `-text` is what makes a
# clone reproduce them: git performs no end-of-line conversion in either direction, on any
# platform, whatever a contributor's `core.autocrlf` happens to be set to.
#
# Measured across every tracked text file at 0.2.6: LF in the root, `.claude`, `.github` and
# `scripts` uniformly; tests 121/132, jnwb 43/48, docs 29/31, artifacts 33/43 -- except
# `skills/`, which is CRLF 16 of 18. That split is real and directional. Whether it should be
# levelled is a separate decision this file deliberately does not take: declaring `eol=lf`
# here would take it silently, by rewriting every CRLF blob on the next checkout.
#
# What is enforced instead is that no single file mixes the two, which is a defect under
# either answer. Harness gate 16 (`scripts/harness_gate.py`) fails on a mixed file.
* -text
